In a proud demonstration of preventive healthcare commitment, Command Hospital (CC) Lucknow conducted a Hand Sanitization Exercise at Shivam Lawns as part of a Pan Armed Forces Medical Services initiative.

The event contributed to a nationwide record attempt for the Largest Hand Sanitization Exercise by Defence Personnel, undertaken across India in association with the Indian Book of Records and Asia Book of Records. The initiative also commemorated 100 years of the Military Nursing Service, honouring a century of dedicated, compassionate care.

A total of 512 uniformed personnel and civilian staff from the Tri-Services, including medical and nursing staff and cadets, collectively performed the WHO-recommended six-step hand sanitization technique using alcohol-based hand rubs, reaffirming adherence to standardized infection prevention protocols.

Brig Vikas Srivastava, Deputy Commandant, Command Hospital (CC) Lucknow, attended and underscored the critical role of hand hygiene in healthcare delivery systems. The coordinated effort reflected AFMS commitment to patient safety, professional excellence and preventive healthcare while contributing to a historic national and Asian record.

